<p class="lg-image-credit">Hinge image sourced from LG’s Australian website.</p>
<p>The gap beside a fridge can make a surprising difference to a kitchen. Too much empty space looks unfinished; too little can leave a door catching the wall. LG’s 436-litre Fit &amp; Max bottom mount range tackles that problem with hinges designed to work close to the surrounding cupboards.</p>

<p><strong>We have seen this fridge hands-on.</strong> Two features stood out: the zero clearance hinges and the dedicated cooling outlet for the door area. This first look explains those features, the four finishes and the installation details worth checking before ordering. We have not measured cooling speed, noise or energy use, or assessed long-term reliability.</p>

<h2 id="a-small-side-gap-with-a-practical-purpose">A small side gap with a practical purpose</h2>
<p>LG calls the design <a href="https://www.lg.com/au/fridge-freezers/bottom-mount/gb-b400mw/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zero Clearance Hinges</a>. The aim is to let the door open beside nearby cabinetry without needing the wide side gap some conventional hinges require. Slim doors and a front vent/exhaust help the appliance sit closer to the surrounding joinery. </p>

<p>The clearances confirmed in our hands-on notes are <strong>4 mm on each side and 9 mm above</strong>. LG’s Australian product page also publishes the 4 mm side recommendation. Adding those gaps to the 697 mm width and 1740 mm height gives a nominal opening of <strong>705 mm wide by 1749 mm high</strong>. Measure the narrowest part of your opening and allow for uneven cupboards, skirting and installation tolerances.</p>

<p>A close fit at the sides does not guarantee the doors will line up with your benchtop. Depth, the rear gap and the position of nearby handles still determine the finished result. Nor does a hinge remove the space the open door needs in front of the fridge.</p>

<h2 id="the-installation-details-to-check">The installation details to check</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://gscs-b2c.lge.com/open/downloadFile?fileId=Ku5jhJkd1wtwzBvNmhPZ9g#page=11" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">owner’s manual linked from LG’s GB-B400MW support page</a> specifies <strong>more than 50 mm behind the appliance</strong>. Keep that rear space in the plan, even though the side gaps are small.</p>

<div class="lg-spec-table"><table><caption>Published dimensions and planning allowances</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Measurement</th><th scope="col">What to allow for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Fridge size, W × H × D</td><td>697 × 1740 × 696 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Side gaps</td><td>4 mm each side, recommended by LG</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rear gap</td><td>More than 50 mm in the linked manual</td></tr>
<tr><td>Top gap</td><td>9 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nominal opening, W × H</td><td>705 × 1749 mm, calculated from the gaps above</td></tr>
<tr><td>Packed size, W × H × D</td><td>739 × 1812 × 772 mm</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<p>Keep the front vent/exhaust unobstructed. Before cabinetry is made, have the installer confirm the complete arrangement for your exact model.</p>

<p>The doors are reversible. LG’s manual says reversal must be carried out by qualified LG Electronics personnel, so arrange the opening direction and any charge before delivery. Choose the direction that gives you a clear route between the fridge and the bench where you unpack groceries.</p>

<p>Check the delivery route as well as the kitchen opening. The packed dimensions matter at narrow doors and stair turns. Our <a href="/guides/fridge-dimensions-australia/">fridge measuring guide</a> includes a worksheet for the opening, access and delivery measurements.</p>

<h2 id="cooling-for-the-food-in-the-door">Cooling for the food in the door</h2>
<p>The second feature that caught our attention was <strong>DoorCooling+</strong>: a dedicated outlet near the top front of the fridge, aimed at the door area. It addresses a practical concern for anyone who regularly reaches for drinks or condiments stored there.</p>

<figure class="lg-detail"><img src="/images/guides/lg-fit-max-door-cooling.webp" alt="Dedicated DoorCooling+ outlet along the top front of the LG fridge compartment." width="800" height="531" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>The DoorCooling+ outlet sits near the front of the compartment. Image sourced from LG’s Australian website.</figcaption></figure>

<p>LG says door cooling starts <strong>15 seconds after the doors close</strong>. That is the start of the airflow, not a promise that a warm bottle will be cold in 15 seconds. We have not timed temperature recovery or compared it with another fridge.</p>

<p>For everyday use, keep the outlet clear when loading taller items. The presence of a cooling feature is less useful if groceries block the airflow or the door is left open while you reorganise the shelves.</p>

<h2 id="four-finishes-and-where-to-find-them">Four finishes, and where to find them</h2>
<p>The range comes in <strong>Matte White (GB-B400MW), Stainless (GB-B400PS), Black (GB-B400BS) and Matte Black (GB-B400MB)</strong>. The Good Guys describes the Black version as Black Steel. Check the full model code when comparing prices, particularly between the two dark finishes.</p>

<p>All four share the published 436-litre capacity, dimensions and 5.5-star energy rating. Choose the finish against your actual cupboard and benchtop colours: a screen cannot show precisely how a surface will look in your kitchen light.</p>

